      "bodyTr": "A month has passed since the prosecution was abolished and the criminal justice system was reorganized to completely separate investigation and prosecution. Under the grand justification of distributing concentrated power through mutual checks and balances among criminal justice agencies to protect citizens' fundamental rights, the hastily implemented \"complete separation of investigative and prosecutorial powers\" has left the field in chaos. Both investigative agencies and the public have been bewildered. The lackluster performance of the Serious Crimes Investigation Agency, which launched under the banner of a \"Korean-style FBI,\" reveals the gap with reality. As of the 30th of last month, nationwide serious crimes investigation units referred only three cases and secured arrests in just five cases over the past month. Due to unclear divisions of responsibility among investigative agencies, complaints and accusations are frequently transferred after being assigned. A \"investigation ping-pong\" between the serious crimes unit and police over a financial company hacking investigation ultimately resulted in police taking over the case. After the reorganization of the criminal justice system, requests for supplementary investigations by the prosecution increased 60.2 percent compared to the same period last year. Because prosecutors lost their investigative authority, even simple typographical errors or wording corrections in warrants must be sent back to police, and additional evidence submitted during trial proceedings cannot be submitted through prosecutors, forcing cases to shuttle between police and prosecutors' offices and inevitably causing further trial delays. Meanwhile, the police, who now hold full authority over general criminal investigations, have demonstrated a lack of expertise. As 9.2 percent of investigative police have no investigation experience at all, and expertise in corporate crimes or tax and stock price manipulation cases is severely lacking, discussions have even emerged about establishing law schools at police academies. All of this was foreseeable and repeatedly pointed out. Political logic has overwhelmed legal logic, and the result is pursuing a speed-driven approach to \"complete separation of investigative and prosecutorial powers\" without preparing measures to fill the various gaps created by the abolition of prosecutorial investigative authority. Even organizational discipline problems emerged, with subordinate regional serious crimes unit heads publicly attacking their superiors. If this state continues, it is clear that only criminals will benefit. Despite the disastrous beginning, measures must be quickly put in place to narrow the gaps in public order and investigation. Supplementary legislation and countermeasures must also be accelerated. If this situation repeats, it is only a matter of time before South Korea's investigative and criminal justice system, which enjoys high international acclaim, collapses.",
