        "bodyTr": "The funeral procession of Choi Bool-am, the \"Father of the Nation,\" takes place today. The significance of his passing extends far beyond the retirement of a veteran actor—it represents the loss of a familiar face that embodied modern Korean history and the lives of ordinary people. He was a figure who showed us what kind of support and what kind of adult we sought in a rapidly changing world. Kim, the chairman of Yangjchon-ri in \"Rural Diary,\" which aired for 22 years from 1980 to 2002, was a dependable father figure who preserved a spiritual homeland for those who had left the countryside due to industrialization and urbanization. As a patriarch, he was stern yet warm-hearted, caring for his family and village neighbors. The authority he wielded as a head of household stemmed from his sense of responsibility toward family and community. His rough manner of speech and his hearty, distinctive laughter carried the weight and affection of fathers of that era. He embodied the ideal image of a family patriarch that the public desired. Detective Park from \"Police Detective,\" which aired for 18 years from 1971 to 1989, confronted his times in the heart of the city. Whenever crimes born of survival pressures—reflecting the shadows of compressed growth—appeared, he pursued criminals relentlessly and apprehended them, yet did not end his mission there. He hated the sin but not the sinner, and he anguished over the circumstances behind crimes and human dignity. Following his mother's wishes, Choi Bool-am's Detective Park never carried a gun or handcuffs even within the drama, becoming yet another symbol of an admirable adult. The rural Chairman Kim and the urban Detective Park reflected two faces of the era while sustaining the hearts of the nation. Choi Bool-am outside the drama was no different. In the documentary \"Korean Table,\" which he hosted for 14 years beginning in 2011, he did not merely taste modest foods from across the country but listened carefully to people's lives. When the \"Choi Bool-am Series,\" which humorously satirized him in the 1990s, became a trend, he accepted it with hearty laughter and broke down barriers with younger generations. His decades-long support for children and youth through the Green Umbrella Foundation proved that his warmth extended beyond words and acting. He was called the \"Father of the Nation\" not merely because he played father roles well. Rather, it was because he was an adult who was stern yet kind, upheld principles yet understood people, and stood beside ordinary citizens rather than preaching from on high, laughing and crying with them. What he left behind is, ultimately, a longing for such an adult.",
